// Original Valus instruction mapped to the 2026–27 HOSA event test plans.
// Clinical and laboratory procedures are simulation learning, not authorization to practice.
import { courseFrom } from './courses-two.js';

export const COURSES_FOUR = {};
// References for these events live with the shared builder in courses-two.js.
const course = (name, units) => { COURSES_FOUR[name] = courseFrom(name, units); };

course('World Health & Disparities',[
 ['Unit 1 · What is health','“What is health” makes up 20% of the World Health & Disparities test plan. Health is shaped by far more than medical care: income, education, environment and policy all influence how long and how well people live.',[
  ['Defining and measuring health','The World Health Organization defines health as a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease. Population health is measured with indicators such as life expectancy, infant mortality, maternal mortality and disability-adjusted life years, which combine years lost to early death and years lived with disability. Data sources such as Our World in Data allow comparisons across countries and over time.','Every indicator answers a specific question. Infant mortality, deaths before age one per 1,000 live births, is sensitive to maternal health, sanitation and access to care, so it is often used to compare health systems. Life expectancy at birth can be pulled down sharply by deaths in early childhood. When comparing countries, check the year, definitions and data quality, and remember an average can hide large differences within a country.',['Country A has a higher life expectancy than Country B, but Country B’s infant mortality has fallen rapidly in ten years. What can you conclude, and what more would you want to know?','Country A currently has better overall survival, but Country B is improving quickly, possibly through better maternal care or sanitation. You would want trends over time, differences within each country, and causes of death to understand the full picture.'],[['WHO definition','Health includes physical, mental and social well-being.',['well-being','physical','social']],['Infant mortality','Deaths before age one per 1,000 live births.',['infant','1,000','births']],['DALYs','Combine years lost to early death and years lived with disability.',['daly','disability','years']]],['Infant mortality is usually expressed per:',['1,000 live births','100 adults','1 million households','10 hospitals'],'It counts deaths before age one per 1,000 live births.'],['Which measure combines early death and years lived with disability?',['Disability-adjusted life years','Crude birth rate','Hospital bed count','Median income'],'DALYs combine mortality and morbidity.']],
  ['Social determinants of health','Social determinants of health are the conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work and age. They include economic stability, education access and quality, health care access and quality, neighborhood and built environment, and social and community context. These conditions influence exposure to risks, access to resources and the ability to follow medical advice.','Determinants act upstream of individual choices. A patient told to eat fresh vegetables may live far from a grocery store, and a worker without paid sick leave may not be able to attend appointments. Addressing determinants requires partnerships beyond clinics, such as housing, transportation and education policy. Screening patients for social needs and connecting them with community resources is one way health systems respond.',['A clinic notices many patients with uncontrolled diabetes live in the same neighborhood. Identify likely social determinants and one upstream response.','Possible determinants include limited access to healthy food, unsafe places to exercise, transportation barriers and financial stress. An upstream response might be partnering with community groups to bring a farmers’ market or food program to the neighborhood.'],[['Economic stability','Income and employment affect resources for health.',['income','economic','employ']],['Built environment','Housing, transport and food access shape daily risks.',['environment','housing','food']],['Upstream action','Addressing root conditions beyond the clinic.',['upstream','root','policy']]],['Which is a social determinant of health?',['Access to safe housing','Blood type','Eye color','Height'],'Housing is a condition shaped by social and economic factors.'],['An upstream intervention addresses:',['Root social conditions that cause poor health','Only individual prescriptions','Only hospital furniture','Genetic mutations'],'Upstream action targets causes before illness develops.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Health disparities and diversity','Health disparities and diversity are the largest domain at 40% of the test plan. Expect questions about how and why health outcomes differ between groups.',[
  ['Understanding health disparities','A health disparity is a difference in health that is closely linked to social, economic or environmental disadvantage. Disparities affect groups based on race or ethnicity, income, education, geography, disability, gender, sexual orientation and other characteristics. Health equity means everyone has a fair opportunity to attain their highest level of health. Equality gives everyone the same resources; equity gives resources according to need.','Disparities are rarely caused by one factor. Structural racism, residential segregation, unequal school funding and wage gaps can concentrate disadvantage across generations. Rural communities may face hospital closures and long travel times. Measuring disparities requires disaggregated data, broken down by group, because averages hide gaps. Intersectionality recognizes that people belong to multiple groups whose effects combine.',['National average infant mortality looks stable, but disaggregated data shows one group’s rate is twice another’s. Why does disaggregation matter, and what might explain the gap?','Averages hide inequities that disaggregated data reveals. The gap may reflect differences in access to prenatal care, chronic stress, structural racism, insurance coverage and quality of care rather than biology alone.'],[['Health disparity','A health difference linked to social disadvantage.',['disparit','differ','disadvantage']],['Equity vs equality','Equity allocates resources according to need.',['equity','need','fair']],['Disaggregated data','Data broken down by group reveals hidden gaps.',['disaggregat','group','data']]],['Which best describes health equity?',['Fair opportunity for everyone to reach their highest level of health','Identical treatment regardless of need','Lower costs for hospitals','Equal height for all'],'Equity focuses on fairness and need.'],['Why is disaggregated data important for studying disparities?',['Averages can hide differences between groups','It reduces sample size','It removes all bias','It is required only for budgets'],'Breaking data down reveals inequities.']],
  ['Global disparities and infectious and chronic disease','Health varies widely between countries. Low-income countries often carry a larger burden of infectious diseases such as malaria, tuberculosis and diarrheal disease, along with maternal and neonatal deaths. As incomes rise, non-communicable diseases such as heart disease, diabetes and cancer become leading causes of death, a pattern called the epidemiologic transition. Many countries now face a double burden of both.','Clean water, sanitation, vaccines and skilled birth attendance have saved millions of lives at relatively low cost. Tuberculosis remains a leading infectious killer, linked to poverty, crowding, HIV and limited access to diagnosis and treatment. Global inequities in access to medicines and vaccines were highlighted during recent pandemics. Global health efforts work best when led with local communities rather than imposed from outside.',['A middle-income country reduces child deaths from diarrhea but sees rising diabetes rates. Explain this using the epidemiologic transition.','Improved sanitation, oral rehydration and vaccines reduced infectious deaths, so people live longer. Urbanization, diet changes and less physical activity raise chronic disease, creating a double burden that the health system must address.'],[['Epidemiologic transition','Shift from infectious to chronic disease as leading causes.',['transition','chronic','infectious']],['Double burden','Infectious and chronic diseases coexist.',['double','burden','both']],['Low-cost interventions','Water, sanitation and vaccines prevent many deaths.',['water','sanitation','vaccin']]],['The shift from infectious to chronic diseases as leading causes of death is called:',['The epidemiologic transition','The demographic dividend','Herd immunity','Brain drain'],'This shift accompanies development and aging populations.'],['Which disease remains a leading infectious killer strongly linked to poverty and crowding?',['Tuberculosis','Osteoarthritis','Cataracts','Gout'],'Tuberculosis spreads readily in crowded conditions.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Disparities in treatment and decisions','Disparities in treatment and medical decisions account for 20% of the test plan. Even after people reach care, the care they receive can differ.',[
  ['Bias, communication and trust','Implicit bias refers to unconscious associations that can influence decisions. Studies have documented differences in pain treatment, referral and procedure rates between groups with similar clinical needs. Communication barriers, including limited English proficiency and low health literacy, affect understanding and outcomes. Trained medical interpreters improve accuracy compared with untrained family members.','Mistrust of health care can be rooted in historical abuses and present experiences of discrimination. Rebuilding trust requires consistent respectful care, transparency, diverse workforces and community partnerships. Clinicians can reduce bias by using standardized protocols, checking decisions against guidelines and reflecting on assumptions. Teach-back, asking patients to explain instructions in their own words, confirms understanding without blaming the patient.',['A patient with limited English is offered help from their 12-year-old child to interpret a diagnosis. What should the clinician do instead and why?','Use a trained medical interpreter. A child may not know medical terms, may omit information, and should not carry that burden. Professional interpretation improves accuracy, confidentiality and safety.'],[['Implicit bias','Unconscious associations affecting decisions.',['implicit','bias','unconscious']],['Medical interpreter','Trained interpretation improves accuracy and safety.',['interpret','trained','language']],['Teach-back','Patients explain instructions in their own words.',['teach-back','explain','understand']]],['Which method confirms a patient understood instructions?',['Teach-back','Speaking louder','Giving more pamphlets only','Using more jargon'],'Teach-back checks understanding directly.'],['Why are trained medical interpreters preferred over family members?',['They improve accuracy and protect confidentiality','They are always relatives','They decide treatment','They replace consent'],'Professional interpreters reduce errors.']],
  ['Access, insurance and quality of care','Access depends on availability of services, affordability, accommodation such as hours and transport, and acceptability to patients. Uninsured and underinsured people are more likely to delay care and present with advanced disease. Quality of care can vary between facilities serving different communities. Clinical trials have historically under-enrolled women, older adults and racial and ethnic minorities, which limits knowledge about how treatments work across groups.','Barriers combine: a patient may have insurance but no nearby specialist, or a nearby clinic that does not accept their plan. Telehealth can expand access but depends on broadband and digital skills. Increasing diversity in clinical research requires building trust, reducing participation burdens such as travel, and engaging communities in research design.',['A rural patient with insurance still waits months for a specialist. Using the dimensions of access, explain the barriers and one solution.','The barrier is availability: few specialists nearby. Travel distance affects accommodation. Telehealth visits or visiting specialist clinics could improve availability if broadband and scheduling support are available.'],[['Availability','Services must exist where people live.',['availab','exist','nearby']],['Affordability','Costs and insurance shape whether care is used.',['afford','cost','insur']],['Research diversity','Trials should include the people treatments will serve.',['trial','diverse','represent']]],['Which barrier describes a lack of nearby providers?',['Availability','Acceptability','Literacy','Confidentiality'],'Availability concerns whether services exist nearby.'],['Why does diversity in clinical trials matter?',['Treatments may work differently across groups','Trials are only for marketing','It shortens all trials','It removes the need for consent'],'Diverse enrollment improves knowledge for all patients.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Reducing health disparities','Reducing health disparities is 20% of the test plan. Effective solutions combine policy, community action and changes inside health systems.',[
  ['Policy and community strategies','Policies that reduce disparities include expanding insurance coverage, raising income supports, investing in education and housing, and enforcing anti-discrimination laws. Community health workers, trusted members of the communities they serve, connect people with care, provide education and address social needs. Community-based participatory research involves community members as partners in every stage of research.','Interventions should be evaluated for whether they narrow gaps, not just improve averages. A program that helps already advantaged groups more may widen disparities even while overall outcomes improve. Cultural and linguistic tailoring, community ownership and sustainable funding increase success. Health in all policies encourages decision-makers in transportation, housing and education to consider health effects.',['A city launches a free screening program, but uptake is lowest in the neighborhood with the highest disease rates. Suggest changes to reduce the disparity.','Partner with community health workers from the neighborhood, offer screening at trusted local sites and convenient hours, provide materials in relevant languages, and remove transportation barriers. Track uptake by neighborhood to confirm the gap narrows.'],[['Community health workers','Trusted community members linking people with care.',['community','worker','trust']],['Narrowing gaps','Programs should be judged by whether disparities shrink.',['gap','narrow','disparit']],['Health in all policies','Considering health in non-health sectors.',['all policies','sector','housing']]],['Community health workers are effective largely because they:',['Share culture and trust with the community','Prescribe all medications','Replace hospitals','Avoid social needs'],'Trust and shared experience improve engagement.'],['A program improves average health but widens the gap between groups. This means:',['It may increase disparities despite overall gains','It has eliminated disparities','It needs no evaluation','The data must be wrong'],'Equity requires examining gaps, not only averages.']],
  ['Health system strategies and cultural humility','Health systems reduce disparities by collecting race, ethnicity, language and social-needs data, stratifying quality measures by group, and setting equity goals. Language access services, accessible facilities for people with disabilities, and flexible scheduling remove barriers. Cultural humility is a lifelong commitment to self-reflection, learning from patients and addressing power imbalances, rather than assuming expertise in another culture.','Workforce diversity is linked to improved communication and trust. Pipeline programs, including student organizations such as HOSA, help students from under-represented backgrounds enter health careers. Accountability matters: equity dashboards make gaps visible and help leaders track progress. Engaging patients in designing services ensures solutions fit real needs.',['A hospital finds its readmission rate is much higher for patients with limited English. Propose a system-level plan to address it.','Stratify readmission data by language, provide interpreter-supported discharge teaching with teach-back, translated written instructions, and follow-up calls in the patient’s language. Track readmissions by language group to measure whether the gap closes.'],[['Stratified measures','Quality data broken down by patient group.',['stratif','data','group']],['Cultural humility','Ongoing self-reflection and learning from patients.',['humility','reflect','learn']],['Workforce diversity','Diverse staff improve communication and trust.',['workforce','diverse','pipeline']]],['Cultural humility emphasizes:',['Lifelong self-reflection and learning from patients','Memorizing facts about every culture','Avoiding patient input','Treating everyone identically'],'Humility centers learning and power awareness.'],['Stratifying quality data by patient group helps a hospital:',['Identify disparities in care','Reduce the number of patients','Avoid reporting','Increase prices'],'Stratification makes gaps visible.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Biotechnology',[
 ['Unit 1 · Industry, health and regulation','Biotechnology industry practices and careers, biotechnology in health, and government regulation are each 4% of the Biotechnology test plan. These domains provide context for the laboratory techniques that make up most of the test.',[
  ['The biotechnology industry, careers and health applications','Biotechnology uses living systems and molecules to make products and solve problems. Health applications include recombinant proteins such as human insulin, vaccines, monoclonal antibodies, gene therapies and diagnostic tests. The first commercial recombinant human insulin was produced in Escherichia coli. Careers include research associates, quality control analysts, manufacturing technicians, bioinformaticians and regulatory affairs specialists.','Product development moves from research to development to manufacturing. Good manufacturing practice requires documented, consistent processes so every batch meets specifications. Quality control tests products, while quality assurance oversees systems that ensure quality. Systems biology fields study complete sets of molecules: genomics studies the full DNA, proteomics all proteins, and metabolomics small-molecule metabolites.',['A company scales up production of a recombinant protein that worked in a small research batch. Which roles and practices become important?','Manufacturing technicians follow documented procedures under good manufacturing practice, quality control analysts test each batch, and quality assurance ensures the system remains consistent. Regulatory specialists prepare data for approval.'],[['Recombinant proteins','Proteins made by organisms carrying inserted genes.',['recombinant','protein','insulin']],['Good manufacturing practice','Documented, consistent processes for every batch.',['manufactur','gmp','consistent']],['Omics fields','Genomics, proteomics and metabolomics study complete molecular sets.',['genomic','proteomic','omics']]],['Which field studies the full complement of DNA in a cell?',['Genomics','Proteomics','Metabolomics','Microbiomics'],'Genomics focuses on the complete genome.'],['Which organism was first used commercially to produce human insulin?',['Escherichia coli','Saccharomyces only','Staphylococcus','Epstein-Barr virus'],'Recombinant human insulin was first produced in E. coli.']],
  ['Government regulation and bioethics','In the United States, the Food and Drug Administration regulates drugs, biologics and medical devices, the Environmental Protection Agency regulates certain genetically modified organisms released into the environment, and the Department of Agriculture oversees agricultural biotechnology. Clinical trials progress through phases: phase 1 tests safety in a small group, phase 2 explores effectiveness and dosing, phase 3 confirms effectiveness in large groups, and phase 4 monitors after approval.','Institutional review boards protect human research participants, and institutional biosafety committees review work with recombinant DNA. Ethical questions in biotechnology include genetic privacy, equitable access to expensive therapies, and the limits of germline editing. Patents encourage innovation by granting temporary exclusive rights but can raise costs and limit access.',['A new gene therapy shows promise in phase 2. What must still happen before approval, and what ethical issue might arise after approval?','A larger phase 3 trial must confirm effectiveness and safety before regulatory review. After approval, very high prices may raise equity concerns about who can access the therapy, and phase 4 monitoring continues.'],[['FDA','Regulates drugs, biologics and medical devices.',['fda','drug','biologic']],['Clinical trial phases','Phases 1 to 4 move from safety to post-approval monitoring.',['phase','trial','safety']],['Oversight committees','IRBs and biosafety committees protect people and the environment.',['irb','biosafety','review']]],['Which clinical trial phase first tests safety in a small group?',['Phase 1','Phase 3','Phase 4','Phase 2 only'],'Phase 1 focuses on safety and dosing in few participants.'],['Which agency regulates biologics such as vaccines in the United States?',['FDA','OSHA','FAA','FCC'],'The FDA regulates biologics.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Laboratory skills and microbiology','Basic laboratory skills are 14% of the test plan, including PPE, preparing solutions and pipetting, and microbiology and cell culture are 12%. These skills also appear in the practical round.',[
  ['Lab safety, solutions and pipetting','Personal protective equipment includes lab coats, gloves and eye protection matched to the hazard. Safety data sheets describe chemical hazards and handling. Preparing solutions requires calculation and accurate measurement: mass = concentration × volume × formula weight for molar solutions, and C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ for dilutions. Micropipettes measure small volumes; a P20 measures up to 20 µL, a P200 up to 200 µL, and a P1000 up to 1,000 µL.','Accuracy depends on technique. Choose the smallest pipette that holds the volume, press to the first stop to draw liquid, and to the second stop to expel it fully. Hold the pipette vertically when drawing, use a fresh tip for each sample to prevent contamination, and never exceed the pipette’s range. Balances must be tared before weighing. Label every solution with contents, concentration, date and preparer.',['Practice: prepare 100 mL of 0.5 M NaCl (formula weight 58.44 g/mol). Calculate the mass and describe the procedure.','Mass = 0.5 mol/L × 0.1 L × 58.44 g/mol = 2.92 g. Weigh 2.92 g on a tared balance, dissolve in less than 100 mL of water, then bring the final volume to 100 mL and label the container.'],[['Molar solution','Mass equals molarity × volume × formula weight.',['molar','mass','formula']],['Pipette technique','First stop to draw, second stop to expel.',['stop','pipett','draw']],['Contamination control','Fresh tips and proper PPE protect samples and people.',['tip','contamin','ppe']]],['Which micropipette is best for measuring 15 µL?',['P20','P1000','P200','A 10 mL serological pipette'],'Use the smallest pipette whose range includes the volume.'],['When drawing liquid with a micropipette, you press to the:',['First stop','Second stop','Tip ejector','Volume dial only'],'The first stop draws the set volume; the second expels fully.']],
  ['Microbiology and cell culture','Bacteria are prokaryotes without a nucleus. Aseptic technique prevents contamination of cultures and protects workers: work near a flame or in a biosafety cabinet, sterilize loops, and minimize open container time. Bacteria grow in phases: lag, exponential (log), stationary and death. Mammalian cell culture grows cells in sterile media with nutrients and serum at controlled temperature and carbon dioxide levels.','Biosafety levels range from BSL-1 for organisms not known to cause disease in healthy adults to BSL-4 for dangerous exotic agents. Autoclaving uses pressurized steam to sterilize media and waste. Gram staining classifies bacteria by cell wall: Gram-positive bacteria retain crystal violet and appear purple, while Gram-negative bacteria appear pink. Counting colonies on plates from serial dilutions estimates the original cell concentration.',['Practice: a 10⁻⁵ dilution plated with 0.1 mL produces 50 colonies. Estimate colony-forming units per mL in the original culture.','CFU/mL = 50 ÷ (0.1 mL × 10⁻⁵) = 50 ÷ 10⁻⁶ = 5 × 10⁷ CFU/mL.'],[['Aseptic technique','Practices that prevent contamination.',['aseptic','steril','contamin']],['Growth phases','Lag, log, stationary and death phases.',['lag','log','stationary']],['Gram stain','Purple Gram-positive versus pink Gram-negative cells.',['gram','purple','pink']]],['During which phase do bacteria divide most rapidly?',['Exponential (log) phase','Lag phase','Stationary phase','Death phase'],'Cells double at a constant rate in log phase.'],['Gram-positive bacteria appear what color after Gram staining?',['Purple','Pink','Green','Colorless'],'They retain crystal violet.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · DNA, transformation and PCR','DNA structure and analysis is 14% of the test plan, bacterial transformation 10% and polymerase chain reaction 14%. Together these form the core of molecular biotechnology.',[
  ['DNA structure and gel electrophoresis','DNA is a double helix of two antiparallel strands. Each nucleotide contains a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group and a nitrogenous base. Adenine pairs with thymine through two hydrogen bonds, and guanine pairs with cytosine through three. Restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific recognition sequences. Agarose gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size: DNA is negatively charged and moves toward the positive electrode, with smaller fragments traveling farther.','A DNA ladder of known sizes allows estimation of fragment sizes. Higher-percentage gels resolve small fragments better. DNA must be stained to be visible, and stains should be handled with appropriate PPE. Interpreting a gel means comparing band positions with the ladder and expected results: a single band suggests one fragment size, while multiple bands reveal several fragments, such as after a restriction digest.',['A plasmid is cut with one restriction enzyme that recognizes two sites. How many bands should appear on a gel, and how would you estimate their sizes?','A circular plasmid cut at two sites produces two linear fragments, so two bands should appear. Compare each band’s migration distance with the DNA ladder to estimate its size.'],[['Base pairing','A with T and G with C through hydrogen bonds.',['pair','hydrogen','base']],['Restriction enzymes','Cut DNA at specific recognition sequences.',['restriction','cut','sequence']],['Electrophoresis','DNA moves toward the positive pole; smaller fragments move farther.',['gel','positive','smaller']]],['Which bonds hold complementary base pairs together?',['Hydrogen bonds','Peptide bonds','Ionic bonds between sugars','Disulfide bonds'],'Base pairs are joined by hydrogen bonds.'],['In gel electrophoresis, DNA migrates toward the:',['Positive electrode','Negative electrode','Top of the gel','Loading well'],'Negatively charged DNA moves toward the positive anode.']],
  ['Bacterial transformation and PCR','Transformation introduces foreign DNA, often a plasmid, into bacteria. Cells are made competent, for example with calcium chloride, then heat shocked to take up DNA. Plasmids carry a selectable marker such as an antibiotic resistance gene; only transformed cells grow on antibiotic plates. Polymerase chain reaction amplifies a DNA region through repeated cycles of denaturation near 95 °C, primer annealing around 50–65 °C, and extension near 72 °C by a heat-stable DNA polymerase.','Controls make results interpretable. In transformation, a plate without plasmid on antibiotic media should show no growth, confirming the antibiotic works. In PCR, a negative control without template detects contamination. Each PCR cycle can double the target, so after n cycles there may be up to 2ⁿ copies. Primers define which region is amplified, and their design determines specificity.',['After transformation, the plasmid plate on ampicillin shows colonies, but so does the no-plasmid control on ampicillin. What does this suggest?','The control should show no growth. Colonies on it suggest the antibiotic was inactive or too low, or cells were contaminated with resistant bacteria, so the results cannot be trusted until repeated.'],[['Selectable marker','An antibiotic resistance gene identifies transformed cells.',['marker','antibiotic','resist']],['PCR cycle','Denature, anneal and extend to copy DNA.',['denatur','anneal','extend']],['Controls','Negative controls reveal contamination or failed selection.',['control','negative','contamin']]],['What is the purpose of heat shock in bacterial transformation?',['To help cells take up plasmid DNA','To kill all bacteria','To denature proteins for gels','To stain colonies'],'Heat shock helps competent cells take up DNA.'],['During which PCR step do primers bind to the template?',['Annealing','Denaturation','Extension','Electrophoresis'],'Primers anneal at a lower temperature after strands separate.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Proteins and immunological applications','Protein structure, function and analysis is 14% of the test plan and immunological applications 10%. Many diagnostic and therapeutic products depend on proteins and antibodies.',[
  ['Protein structure and analysis','Proteins are chains of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, secondary structure includes alpha helices and beta sheets formed by hydrogen bonds, tertiary structure is the overall three-dimensional fold, and quaternary structure is the arrangement of multiple chains. Function depends on shape; heat, pH extremes and some chemicals can denature proteins. SDS-PAGE separates proteins by size after they are denatured and coated with negative charge.','Enzymes are proteins that speed reactions by lowering activation energy, and their activity depends on temperature and pH. Protein concentration can be measured with spectrophotometry, using a standard curve of known concentrations. Chromatography purifies proteins based on properties such as size, charge or specific binding. Comparing results to a standard curve requires the unknown to fall within the curve’s range.',['An enzyme works well at 37 °C but loses activity after being heated to 80 °C, even when cooled again. Explain using protein structure.','High heat disrupted the bonds maintaining the enzyme’s three-dimensional shape, denaturing it. Because function depends on shape, the active site no longer works, and the change is often irreversible.'],[['Levels of structure','Primary sequence through quaternary arrangement.',['primary','tertiary','structure']],['Denaturation','Loss of shape and function from heat or pH.',['denatur','shape','heat']],['SDS-PAGE','Separates denatured proteins by size.',['sds','size','separat']]],['What bond links amino acids in a protein chain?',['Peptide bond','Hydrogen bond only','Phosphodiester bond','Glycosidic bond'],'Peptide bonds join amino acids.'],['Which technique separates proteins mainly by size after denaturation?',['SDS-PAGE','PCR','Transformation','Autoclaving'],'SDS gives proteins uniform charge so size drives migration.']],
  ['Antibodies, ELISA and immunological tools','Antibodies are Y-shaped proteins produced by B cells that bind specific antigens. Monoclonal antibodies come from a single clone and recognize one epitope, making them useful in diagnostics and therapy. ELISA, enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, detects antigens or antibodies using an enzyme-linked antibody that produces a color change. Vaccines train the immune system to recognize antigens without causing disease.','Interpreting an ELISA requires controls: a positive control confirms the assay works, and a negative control sets the baseline. More color generally indicates more target present, measured with a plate reader. Washing steps remove unbound material; poor washing can produce false positives. Rapid tests, such as lateral flow assays, use the same antigen-antibody principles in a portable format.',['In an ELISA, every well, including the negative control, turns strongly colored. What went wrong and what should be done?','The negative control should stay near baseline. Strong color everywhere suggests inadequate washing, contaminated reagents or excess enzyme-linked antibody. The assay must be repeated with careful washing and fresh reagents.'],[['Antibody specificity','Each antibody binds a specific antigen or epitope.',['antibod','specific','antigen']],['ELISA','Enzyme-linked antibodies produce color proportional to target.',['elisa','enzyme','color']],['Assay controls','Positive and negative controls validate results.',['control','positive','negative']]],['Monoclonal antibodies are produced by:',['A single clone of cells and recognize one epitope','Many different cell types with mixed targets','Bacteria only','Plants exclusively'],'Monoclonal means derived from one clone.'],['In an ELISA, what creates the visible signal?',['An enzyme reacting with substrate','DNA polymerase','Gel electrophoresis','Heat shock'],'The enzyme converts substrate to a colored product.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Clinical Lab Science (Medical Lab Scientist)',[
 ['Unit 1 · Professions, safety and lab math','Clinical lab professions are 8% of the CLS/MLS test plan, biological safety 12%, and laboratory math 6%. Laboratory results influence a large share of medical decisions, so accuracy and safety come first.',[
  ['Laboratory professions and biological safety','Clinical laboratory roles include phlebotomy technicians, who collect blood specimens; medical laboratory technicians, typically with an associate degree; medical laboratory scientists, typically with a bachelor’s degree; and pathologists, physicians who interpret tissues and oversee laboratories. Standard precautions treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious. PPE includes gloves, lab coats and eye and face protection; N95 respirators filter at least 95% of airborne particles of a specified size.','The chain of infection links an infectious agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host; breaking any link prevents infection. Work surfaces are often disinfected with a 10% bleach solution before and after use. Sharps go immediately into puncture-resistant containers and are never recapped by hand. Exposure incidents must be reported promptly so post-exposure evaluation can begin.',['A laboratory worker is splashed in the eye with a patient specimen. Describe the immediate actions and the reason for each.','Flush the eye at an eyewash station for the recommended time to remove the material, report the exposure to a supervisor immediately, and follow the post-exposure plan so testing and preventive treatment can begin quickly.'],[['Standard precautions','Treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious.',['standard','precaution','infectious']],['Chain of infection','Breaking any link prevents transmission.',['chain','link','transmis']],['Exposure response','Immediate first aid and prompt reporting.',['exposure','report','flush']]],['What does the 95 in an N95 respirator indicate?',['It filters at least 95% of airborne particles of a specified size','It is effective 95% of the time worn','It should be worn 95% of the shift','It filters 95% of all particles of any size'],'N95 respirators filter at least 95% of test particles.'],['Which disinfectant is commonly used on laboratory work surfaces?',['10% bleach solution','40% alcohol','Tap water only','Sugar solution'],'Diluted bleach is a common surface disinfectant.']],
  ['Laboratory math and quality control','Laboratory math includes dilutions, concentration calculations, unit conversions and statistics for quality control. A dilution ratio such as 1:5 means 1 part sample in 5 total parts. Results from diluted samples are multiplied by the dilution factor. Quality control uses control samples with known values; the mean and standard deviation define acceptable ranges, and Levey-Jennings charts plot control results over time.','Westgard rules interpret control results: one value beyond ±3 standard deviations or two consecutive values beyond ±2 standard deviations on the same side suggest an error, and patient results should not be released until the problem is solved. A shift is a sudden change in control values, while a trend is a gradual drift, often from deteriorating reagents. Accuracy is closeness to the true value; precision is reproducibility.',['Practice: a glucose sample was diluted 1:4 because it exceeded the analyzer range, and the diluted result is 150 mg/dL. What is the reported result, and why must controls be checked?','150 × 4 = 600 mg/dL. Controls must be within acceptable limits so the result can be trusted before it is reported.'],[['Dilution factor','Multiply the diluted result by the dilution factor.',['dilution','factor','multiply']],['Levey-Jennings chart','Plots control values against standard deviation limits.',['levey','chart','control']],['Accuracy vs precision','True value versus reproducibility.',['accura','precis','reproduc']]],['A control value falls beyond +3 standard deviations. What should happen?',['Hold patient results and investigate','Report all patient results anyway','Ignore one value','Delete the control'],'A 3SD violation signals a likely error.'],['Repeated measurements that agree closely but are far from the true value are:',['Precise but not accurate','Accurate and precise','Accurate but not precise','Neither precise nor reproducible'],'Precision is agreement; accuracy is closeness to truth.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Hematology and immunology','Hematology and hemostasis are 14% of the test plan and immunology 16%, the largest single domain.',[
  ['Hematology and hemostasis','A complete blood count measures red blood cells, hemoglobin, hematocrit, white blood cells and platelets. Red cell indices, such as mean corpuscular volume, help classify anemias: microcytic anemia often results from iron deficiency, while macrocytic anemia may result from vitamin B12 or folate deficiency. A differential counts white cell types: neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils and basophils. Hemostasis involves vascular constriction, platelet plug formation and the coagulation cascade.','Coagulation tests include prothrombin time with INR, which monitors warfarin therapy, and activated partial thromboplastin time, which assesses the intrinsic pathway and heparin therapy. Specimen handling affects results: coagulation tubes must be filled correctly because the citrate-to-blood ratio matters. A clotted EDTA tube invalidates a CBC. Elevated neutrophils often suggest bacterial infection, while elevated eosinophils may suggest allergy or parasitic infection.',['Practice: a CBC shows low hemoglobin and low mean corpuscular volume. What type of anemia is suggested and what follow-up tests might be ordered?','Low MCV indicates microcytic anemia, commonly from iron deficiency. Iron studies such as serum ferritin could confirm, and the clinical team would look for the cause.'],[['MCV','Red cell size classifies anemias.',['mcv','size','anemia']],['Differential','Counts each type of white blood cell.',['differential','neutrophil','white']],['Coagulation testing','PT/INR and aPTT assess clotting pathways.',['pt','inr','clot']]],['Which test is commonly used to monitor warfarin therapy?',['PT with INR','Hemoglobin A1c','Urine specific gravity','Gram stain'],'PT/INR monitors warfarin anticoagulation.'],['An elevated eosinophil count may suggest:',['Allergy or parasitic infection','Iron deficiency','Dehydration only','Normal pregnancy in all cases'],'Eosinophils increase with allergic and parasitic conditions.']],
  ['Immunology and blood banking','The immune system includes innate defenses and adaptive immunity. B cells produce antibodies; T cells coordinate and carry out cell-mediated responses. Serological tests detect antibodies or antigens. In blood banking, ABO and Rh typing determine compatibility: type O negative red cells are the universal donor type for emergencies, and type AB plasma is the universal plasma donor. A crossmatch tests donor cells against recipient serum before transfusion.','Antibodies to ABO antigens occur naturally: a type A person has anti-B antibodies. Giving incompatible red cells can cause an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction, which is why patient identification at collection and transfusion is critical. Rh-negative pregnant people may receive Rh immune globulin to prevent sensitization that could affect future pregnancies. Sensitivity of a test is its ability to detect true positives; specificity is its ability to identify true negatives.',['Practice: a patient with type A blood needs red cells. Which ABO types are compatible, and why?','Type A and type O red cells are compatible because neither carries B antigen, which the recipient’s anti-B antibodies would attack. Type B and AB red cells are incompatible.'],[['ABO antibodies','People have antibodies against ABO antigens they lack.',['abo','antibod','antigen']],['Crossmatch','Tests donor cells with recipient serum before transfusion.',['crossmatch','donor','recipient']],['Sensitivity vs specificity','Detecting true positives versus true negatives.',['sensitiv','specific','true']]],['Which red cell type is considered the universal donor?',['O negative','AB positive','A positive','B negative'],'O negative cells lack A, B and D antigens.'],['A type B person naturally has which antibody?',['Anti-A','Anti-B','Anti-D always','No antibodies'],'People make antibodies to ABO antigens they lack.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Urinalysis and clinical chemistry','Urinalysis is 8% of the test plan and clinical chemistry 14%. These tests evaluate organ function and metabolic status.',[
  ['Urinalysis','A complete urinalysis has three parts: physical examination of color, clarity and specific gravity; chemical examination with reagent strips for pH, protein, glucose, ketones, blood, bilirubin, urobilinogen, nitrite and leukocyte esterase; and microscopic examination for cells, casts and crystals. A first-morning specimen is most concentrated; a clean-catch midstream specimen reduces contamination.','Interpret results in combination. Positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase together suggest a urinary tract infection. Glucose in urine appears when blood glucose exceeds the kidney’s reabsorption threshold, as in uncontrolled diabetes. Ketones appear when the body breaks down fat for energy. Specimens should be tested within about two hours or refrigerated, because bacteria multiply and cells break down at room temperature.',['Practice: a reagent strip shows positive nitrite and leukocyte esterase, and the microscopic exam shows many white cells and bacteria. What do these findings suggest?','Together they suggest a urinary tract infection: nitrite indicates certain bacteria and leukocyte esterase indicates white cells. A culture may be performed to identify the organism and guide treatment.'],[['Three-part urinalysis','Physical, chemical and microscopic examinations.',['physical','chemical','microscop']],['Nitrite and leukocyte esterase','Together suggest urinary tract infection.',['nitrite','leukocyte','infection']],['Specimen timing','Test promptly or refrigerate to prevent changes.',['timing','refrigerat','prompt']]],['Which urine specimen is the most concentrated?',['First-morning specimen','Random afternoon specimen','24-hour average','Post-meal specimen'],'Overnight concentration makes first-morning urine most concentrated.'],['Which result suggests the body is breaking down fat for energy?',['Ketones','Nitrite','Urobilinogen','Clarity'],'Ketones are products of fat metabolism.']],
  ['Clinical chemistry','Clinical chemistry measures substances in blood such as glucose, electrolytes, enzymes, lipids and markers of kidney and liver function. A basic metabolic panel includes glucose, sodium, potassium, chloride, carbon dioxide, blood urea nitrogen and creatinine. Hemoglobin A1c reflects average blood glucose over about three months. Troponin is a sensitive marker of heart muscle injury.','Pre-analytical errors, which happen before testing, are the most common source of lab error. Hemolysis, the rupture of red cells during collection, can falsely raise potassium. Collecting from an arm with an IV infusion can dilute or contaminate results. Fasting requirements apply to some tests, such as certain lipid panels. Reference ranges vary by laboratory and population, so results are interpreted against the lab’s own range.',['Practice: a potassium result is very high, and the specimen is noted as hemolyzed. How should the result be handled?','Hemolysis releases potassium from red cells, so the result may be falsely elevated. The lab should notify the provider that the specimen is compromised and request a new specimen rather than reporting it as a reliable result.'],[['Basic metabolic panel','Glucose, electrolytes and kidney markers.',['metabolic','electrolyt','kidney']],['Pre-analytical error','Errors before testing, like hemolysis, distort results.',['pre-analytical','hemoly','collect']],['Hemoglobin A1c','Reflects average glucose over about three months.',['a1c','average','glucose']]],['Which test reflects average blood glucose over about three months?',['Hemoglobin A1c','Random glucose','Urine ketones','Troponin'],'A1c reflects glycation of hemoglobin over red cell lifespan.'],['Hemolysis during collection can falsely increase:',['Potassium','Sodium in every case','Urine pH','Bacteria counts'],'Red cells contain high potassium.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Microbiology and parasitology','Clinical microbiology is 14% of the test plan and parasitology 8%. These sections identify organisms that cause infection and guide treatment.',[
  ['Clinical microbiology','Clinical microbiology identifies bacteria, fungi and viruses from specimens such as blood, urine, sputum and wounds. Culture grows organisms on media; selective media favor certain organisms and differential media reveal characteristics through color. Gram staining gives a rapid first clue. Antimicrobial susceptibility testing determines which antibiotics inhibit an organism and reports results as susceptible, intermediate or resistant.','Specimen quality determines result quality. Blood cultures should be collected before antibiotics when possible, with careful skin antisepsis to avoid contamination by skin bacteria. Sputum containing many epithelial cells suggests saliva contamination. Antimicrobial resistance is a growing threat; laboratories report resistance patterns that help guide prescribing and infection control.',['Practice: a blood culture grows a common skin organism in only one of four bottles. What might this indicate?','Growth of a skin organism in a single bottle may indicate contamination during collection rather than true infection. The clinical team would consider the patient’s condition and repeat cultures if needed.'],[['Culture media','Selective and differential media support identification.',['media','selective','differential']],['Susceptibility testing','Reports which antibiotics inhibit the organism.',['suscepti','antibiotic','resist']],['Specimen quality','Proper collection prevents contamination.',['specimen','collect','contamin']]],['Susceptibility results are usually reported as:',['Susceptible, intermediate or resistant','Positive or neutral only','Red, yellow or green','High, low or absent'],'These categories guide antibiotic selection.'],['When should blood cultures ideally be collected?',['Before antibiotics are started','Only after a week of antibiotics','Never with skin antisepsis','After discharge'],'Antibiotics can prevent organisms from growing.']],
  ['Parasitology','Parasites include protozoa, single-celled organisms such as Giardia and Plasmodium, and helminths, multicellular worms such as roundworms, tapeworms and flukes. Ectoparasites such as lice live on the body surface. Diagnosis often uses microscopic examination of stool for ova and parasites, blood smears for malaria, and antigen tests. Life cycles involve stages and sometimes intermediate hosts.','Understanding the life cycle explains prevention. Malaria is transmitted by Anopheles mosquitoes, so bed nets and mosquito control reduce infection. Many intestinal parasites spread through the fecal-oral route, so sanitation and hand hygiene are key. Multiple stool specimens may be needed because parasites are shed intermittently. Thick blood smears detect malaria parasites, while thin smears help identify the species.',['Practice: a traveler returns from a malaria-endemic region with cyclic fevers. Which laboratory tests are appropriate and why?','Thick and thin blood smears detect and identify Plasmodium species, and rapid antigen tests may provide quick screening. Repeat smears may be needed if the first is negative and suspicion remains.'],[['Protozoa vs helminths','Single-celled versus multicellular parasites.',['protozo','helminth','worm']],['Life cycle','Stages and hosts explain transmission and prevention.',['life cycle','host','stage']],['Blood smears','Thick smears detect and thin smears identify malaria.',['smear','malaria','blood']]],['Malaria is caused by which type of organism?',['Protozoan (Plasmodium)','Bacterium','Virus','Helminth'],'Plasmodium species are protozoa.'],['Why may several stool specimens be needed for ova and parasite testing?',['Parasites may be shed intermittently','One specimen damages the microscope','Stool is never useful','Parasites only appear at night in stool'],'Intermittent shedding can cause false negatives.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Clinical Nursing',[
 ['Unit 1 · Basic care','Basic care makes up 20% of the Clinical Nursing test plan. Nursing combines assessment, clinical judgment and caring communication. Procedures described here are for simulation learning and must be performed only within training and scope.',[
  ['The nursing process and clinical judgment','The nursing process organizes care in five steps: assessment, nursing diagnosis, planning, implementation and evaluation, often remembered as ADPIE. Assessment gathers subjective data, what the patient reports, and objective data, what the nurse observes or measures. Nursing diagnoses describe patient responses to health problems. Goals should be patient-centered, measurable and time-limited. Reflective practice helps nurses evaluate and improve their own clinical skills.','Prioritization often follows airway, breathing and circulation, then Maslow’s hierarchy, placing physiological and safety needs first. Vital signs include temperature, pulse, respirations, blood pressure, oxygen saturation and pain. Trends matter more than single values: a steadily rising pulse and falling blood pressure can signal deterioration. Alarm fatigue occurs when frequent alarms desensitize staff; individualizing alarm parameters according to unit policy reduces it.',['A postoperative patient’s pulse has risen from 82 to 118 over two hours while blood pressure fell from 128/80 to 96/60. What should the nurse recognize and do?','The trend suggests possible bleeding or shock. The nurse should assess the patient and surgical site, check other signs such as skin color and urine output, and promptly report to the provider using a structured communication tool.'],[['ADPIE','Assessment, diagnosis, planning, implementation, evaluation.',['assess','plan','evaluat']],['Prioritization','Airway, breathing and circulation come first.',['airway','breathing','priorit']],['Trends','Changes over time reveal deterioration.',['trend','change','deteriorat']]],['Information the patient reports, such as pain, is:',['Subjective data','Objective data','A nursing intervention','A medical order'],'Subjective data come from the patient’s report.'],['Which strategy can reduce alarm fatigue on a care unit?',['Individualize alarm parameters according to unit policy','Turn off all alarms','Ignore repeated alarms','Use the same settings for everyone'],'Individualized limits reduce non-actionable alarms.']],
  ['Safety, infection control and comfort','Patient safety includes correct patient identification with two identifiers, fall prevention, and safe patient handling. Hand hygiene is the single most important measure to prevent health care-associated infections. Transmission-based precautions add to standard precautions: contact, droplet and airborne. Pressure injuries develop over bony prominences; repositioning, skin assessment, moisture control and nutrition help prevent them.','Pain is whatever the patient says it is, assessed with appropriate scales and reassessed after interventions. Non-pharmacologic comfort measures include positioning, heat or cold as ordered, distraction and relaxation. Body mechanics, such as a wide base of support and lifting with the legs, protect nurses from injury. Restraints are a last resort, require an order and frequent monitoring.',['A patient with limited mobility has a reddened area on the sacrum that does not blanch. What does this suggest, and what interventions are appropriate?','Non-blanchable redness suggests a stage 1 pressure injury. Relieve pressure with a turning schedule and support surfaces, keep skin clean and dry, support nutrition and hydration, document, and report to the care team.'],[['Patient identification','Use two identifiers before care.',['identif','two','name']],['Hand hygiene','The most important infection prevention measure.',['hand','hygiene','infection']],['Pressure injury prevention','Repositioning, skin care and nutrition.',['pressure','reposition','skin']]],['How many patient identifiers should be used before care?',['At least two','None if the nurse recognizes them','Only the room number','Only the bed number'],'Two identifiers prevent wrong-patient errors.'],['Which precautions are used for an airborne disease such as tuberculosis?',['Airborne precautions with a respirator and negative-pressure room','Contact precautions only','No precautions','Droplet precautions with no mask'],'Airborne pathogens need respirators and special rooms.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Care through the lifespan','Clinical care through the lifespan is 20% of the test plan: maternal and newborn, pediatrics, adulthood and gerontology, mental health, and home, extended, rehabilitation and hospice care.',[
  ['Maternal, newborn and pediatric care','Prenatal care monitors maternal and fetal health. After birth, the Apgar score evaluates heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability and color at one and five minutes. Newborn care includes keeping the infant warm, supporting feeding and safe sleep on the back. Pediatric care adapts to developmental stage: vital sign ranges differ by age, and children may show illness through behavior changes.','Family-centered care involves parents in decisions and comfort. Communication should match development: simple, honest explanations for young children, and privacy and involvement for adolescents. Dehydration can progress quickly in infants; signs include fewer wet diapers, dry mucous membranes and a sunken fontanel. Medication doses in children are often weight-based, increasing the importance of accurate weight.',['An infant has had vomiting for a day and has fewer wet diapers. What assessments should a nurse prioritize?','Assess for dehydration: count wet diapers, check mucous membranes, fontanel, skin turgor, heart rate and level of alertness, weigh the infant, and report findings promptly because infants can deteriorate quickly.'],[['Apgar score','Assesses newborn status at one and five minutes.',['apgar','newborn','minute']],['Developmental communication','Explanations match the child’s stage.',['development','explain','child']],['Pediatric dehydration','Infants show fewer wet diapers and a sunken fontanel.',['dehydrat','diaper','fontanel']]],['When is the Apgar score typically assessed?',['At one and five minutes after birth','Only at discharge','At one week','Before delivery'],'Apgar scoring occurs at one and five minutes.'],['Which finding suggests dehydration in an infant?',['Sunken fontanel','Many wet diapers','Moist mucous membranes','Bulging fontanel only from crying'],'A sunken fontanel can indicate fluid loss.']],
  ['Older adults, mental health and care settings','Older adults often have multiple chronic conditions and medications, increasing risk of falls, delirium and adverse drug events. Mental health nursing uses therapeutic communication, such as open-ended questions, active listening and reflection, and assesses safety including risk of suicide. Care settings include acute hospitals, long-term care, rehabilitation, home health and hospice, each with different goals.','Suicide risk must be taken seriously: ask directly about thoughts of self-harm, ensure safety, and report according to policy. Asking directly does not increase risk. Rehabilitation aims to restore function and independence. Home care requires assessing safety of the environment and caregiver support. Hospice focuses on comfort and dignity at the end of life.',['A patient says, “Everyone would be better off without me.” How should the nurse respond?','Take the statement seriously, ask directly whether the patient is thinking about suicide or self-harm, stay with the patient or ensure safety, and report immediately to the provider according to policy.'],[['Therapeutic communication','Open questions, listening and reflection.',['therapeutic','listen','open']],['Suicide safety','Ask directly and ensure immediate safety.',['suicide','safety','ask']],['Care settings','Goals differ across acute, rehab, home and hospice care.',['setting','rehab','hospice']]],['Which is an example of therapeutic communication?',['“Tell me more about how you are feeling.”','“Don’t worry about it.”','“Why would you think that?”','“Everyone feels that way.”'],'Open-ended prompts encourage expression.'],['Asking a patient directly about suicidal thoughts:',['Is appropriate and does not increase risk','Always causes harm','Should be avoided','Is only allowed for physicians'],'Direct questions support safety assessment.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Systems disorders: cardiopulmonary and metabolic','Clinical care for systems diseases and disorders is the largest domain at 60% of the test plan, covering structure and function, signs and symptoms, assessment, medications, nutrition and fluids, and treatments.',[
  ['Cardiovascular and respiratory disorders','Heart failure reduces the heart’s ability to pump effectively; left-sided failure causes pulmonary congestion with shortness of breath and crackles, while right-sided failure causes peripheral edema and jugular vein distention. Myocardial infarction results from blocked coronary blood flow and often causes chest pain, though women and older adults may have atypical symptoms. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease limits airflow; asthma involves reversible airway inflammation and narrowing.','Nursing assessment includes lung sounds, edema, daily weights and oxygen saturation. A weight gain of about 1 kg in a day can indicate fluid retention in heart failure. Positioning upright eases breathing. Oxygen is delivered as ordered, with caution and monitoring for patients with chronic carbon dioxide retention. Signs of postoperative hemorrhage and shock include a rapid, thready pulse, falling blood pressure, cool clammy skin and restlessness.',['A patient with heart failure gains 1.5 kg overnight and has new crackles in both lungs. Explain the findings and nursing actions.','The rapid weight gain and crackles suggest fluid retention with pulmonary congestion. The nurse should position the patient upright, assess oxygen saturation and breathing, monitor intake and output, and notify the provider promptly.'],[['Left vs right heart failure','Pulmonary congestion versus peripheral edema.',['left','right','edema']],['Daily weights','Sudden gain signals fluid retention.',['weight','gain','fluid']],['Shock signs','Rapid thready pulse, hypotension, cool clammy skin.',['shock','pulse','clammy']]],['Which finding is typical of right-sided heart failure?',['Peripheral edema and jugular vein distention','Crackles only','Bradycardia only','High fever'],'Right-sided failure causes systemic venous congestion.'],['Which signs suggest postoperative hemorrhage and shock?',['Rapid thready pulse, hypotension, cold clammy skin','Slow pulse and warm dry skin','Increased urine output and calm','Hypertension with flushed skin'],'Blood loss causes compensatory tachycardia and poor perfusion.']],
  ['Diabetes, fluids and electrolytes','Diabetes mellitus causes high blood glucose from absent insulin, as in type 1, or insulin resistance with relative deficiency, as in type 2. Hypoglycemia causes shakiness, sweating, confusion and can progress to seizures; it is treated with fast-acting carbohydrate if the patient can swallow safely, per protocol. Hyperglycemia causes thirst, frequent urination and fatigue. Electrolyte imbalances such as high or low potassium can cause dangerous heart rhythms.','Fluid balance assessment includes intake and output, daily weights, skin turgor, mucous membranes and vital signs. Isotonic fluids such as 0.9% saline expand extracellular volume. Patient teaching for diabetes covers glucose monitoring, recognizing symptoms, foot care, nutrition and medications. The rule of fifteen describes giving about 15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate and rechecking glucose after 15 minutes.',['A patient with diabetes becomes sweaty, shaky and confused before lunch. What is likely happening and what should the nurse do?','The signs suggest hypoglycemia. The nurse should check blood glucose, give fast-acting carbohydrate per protocol if the patient can swallow safely, recheck in 15 minutes, and notify the provider.'],[['Hypoglycemia','Shakiness, sweating and confusion from low glucose.',['hypoglyc','low','shak']],['Rule of fifteen','15 g carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes.',['fifteen','carbohydrate','recheck']],['Electrolytes','Potassium imbalance can cause dysrhythmias.',['potassium','electrolyt','rhythm']]],['Which symptom group suggests hypoglycemia?',['Shakiness, sweating and confusion','Thirst and frequent urination','Fever and productive cough','Itching and hives'],'Low glucose triggers adrenergic and neurologic symptoms.'],['Which IV fluid is isotonic?',['0.9% sodium chloride','3% sodium chloride','0.45% sodium chloride','Sterile water'],'Normal saline is isotonic with plasma.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Systems disorders: neuro, renal and surgical care','This unit continues systems disorders with neurological, renal and gastrointestinal conditions and perioperative care.',[
  ['Neurological and renal disorders','Stroke occurs when blood flow to the brain is blocked or a vessel bleeds. Warning signs can be remembered with BE FAST: balance, eyes, face drooping, arm weakness, speech difficulty and time to call emergency services. Neurological assessment includes level of consciousness, pupils and motor strength. Acute kidney injury is a sudden decline in kidney function, while chronic kidney disease develops over time; both affect fluid, electrolytes and medication clearance.','Time matters in stroke because some treatments are effective only within a limited window, so the time symptoms started must be documented. Patients with swallowing difficulty after stroke need a swallow screen before oral intake to prevent aspiration. In kidney disease, monitor intake and output, weight, potassium and signs of fluid overload. After a nephrectomy, monitor urine output and signs of bleeding closely.',['A patient suddenly develops facial drooping and slurred speech. What should the nurse do first?','Recognize possible stroke, activate the emergency response, note the time symptoms began, assess airway and neurological status, and keep the patient nothing by mouth until a swallow screen is done.'],[['BE FAST','Balance, eyes, face, arm, speech, time.',['face','arm','speech']],['Swallow screen','Prevents aspiration after stroke.',['swallow','aspirat','screen']],['Kidney monitoring','Output, weight and potassium guide care.',['kidney','output','potassium']]],['In stroke assessment, why is symptom onset time important?',['Some treatments depend on time since onset','It determines room color','It is only for billing','It is not important'],'Time windows determine treatment options.'],['Before giving oral fluids after a stroke, the nurse should:',['Ensure a swallow screen is completed','Give a large glass of water','Lay the patient flat','Skip assessment'],'Swallow screening prevents aspiration.']],
  ['Perioperative care and gastrointestinal disorders','Perioperative care spans preoperative, intraoperative and postoperative phases. Before surgery, nurses verify consent, identification, allergies, fasting status and site marking. After surgery, priorities include airway, breathing, circulation, pain control and preventing complications such as atelectasis, infection and deep vein thrombosis. Early ambulation, deep breathing and incentive spirometry support recovery.','Gastrointestinal disorders include gastroesophageal reflux disease, peptic ulcers, inflammatory bowel disease and appendicitis. Appendicitis often begins with periumbilical pain that moves to the right lower quadrant. Signs of wound infection include redness, warmth, swelling, drainage and fever. Ostomy care includes assessing the stoma, which should be moist and pink to red, and teaching the patient self-care.',['On postoperative day two, a patient has fever and a red, warm, draining incision. What might this indicate and what actions follow?','The findings suggest surgical site infection. The nurse should assess and document the wound, obtain vital signs, follow aseptic technique for dressing care, and notify the provider, who may order a culture and treatment.'],[['Preoperative checks','Consent, identification, allergies and site marking.',['consent','allerg','site']],['Postoperative complications','Atelectasis, infection and DVT prevention.',['atelectasis','infection','dvt']],['Stoma assessment','A healthy stoma is moist and pink to red.',['stoma','pink','moist']]],['Incentive spirometry after surgery mainly helps prevent:',['Atelectasis','Hypoglycemia','Hearing loss','Pressure injury on heels'],'Deep breathing expands alveoli.'],['A healthy stoma usually appears:',['Moist and pink to red','Dry and black','Pale white','Purple and cold'],'Color and moisture indicate perfusion.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Clinical Specialty',[
 ['Unit 1 · Choosing a career and skill','Clinical Specialty asks you to choose a health career, build a career portfolio, record a skill demonstration and present to judges. Read the full guidelines for current skill restrictions, because the selected skill may not duplicate a skill used in another HOSA event.',[
  ['Researching a health career','Choose a career that genuinely interests you and has at least one clinical skill that can be learned and demonstrated safely by a secondary student. Research the career’s education and training path, licensure or certification, typical duties, work settings, salary and job outlook. Reliable sources include the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook, professional associations and accredited program websites.','Interview or shadow a professional if possible, because real experiences strengthen both your portfolio and your presentation. Keep a source log with full citations as you research. Compare at least two sources for important facts such as salary, since figures vary by region and year. Note the career’s scope of practice so your selected skill fits what that professional actually does.',['You are deciding between two careers. How would you compare them to choose one for Clinical Specialty?','Compare interest, available demonstrable skills that meet the event rules, access to a professional to interview or shadow, and quality of available research sources. Choose the career where you can produce strong evidence and a safe, authentic skill.'],[['Career research','Education, credentials, duties, outlook and salary.',['education','credential','outlook']],['Reliable sources','Government data, professional associations and accredited programs.',['source','reliable','bls']],['Scope fit','The skill must match what the professional actually does.',['scope','skill','fit']]],['Which source is most reliable for job outlook data?',['Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook','An anonymous social media post','A video game','An advertisement for a product'],'The OOH publishes official employment projections.'],['Why should the selected skill match the career’s scope of practice?',['Judges evaluate whether it is authentic to the career','It makes the video longer','Any skill is acceptable for any career','Scope does not matter'],'The skill must represent the chosen career.']],
  ['Selecting and learning a clinical skill','A strong skill is safe, clearly defined, appropriate to the career and teachable in steps. Break the skill into a written procedure: preparation, hand hygiene and identification, communication with the patient, step-by-step actions, safety measures, and completion steps such as documentation. Use authoritative references for the procedure and have a qualified professional review your technique if possible.','Practice deliberately: perform the skill slowly with a checklist, get feedback, and repeat until the steps are smooth and correct. Infection control and patient communication are part of the skill, not extras. Choose a mannequin or a volunteer appropriately and follow safety rules. Recording practice sessions lets you review your technique the way judges will see it.',['Outline the written procedure structure for a skill demonstration, explaining why each part matters.','Preparation ensures supplies are ready; hand hygiene and identification protect the patient; explaining the procedure obtains cooperation and consent; the ordered steps show technical accuracy; safety measures prevent harm; and documentation completes professional care.'],[['Procedure steps','A clear sequence from preparation to documentation.',['step','sequence','procedure']],['Safety and infection control','Hand hygiene and safe technique are part of the skill.',['hygiene','safety','infection']],['Deliberate practice','Repeated practice with feedback builds accuracy.',['practice','feedback','repeat']]],['Which step should come early in nearly every clinical skill?',['Hand hygiene and patient identification','Documentation','Cleaning up','Leaving the room'],'Hand hygiene and identification come before patient contact.'],['Why record practice sessions?',['To review technique as judges will see it','To avoid practicing','To skip feedback','To replace the written procedure'],'Video review reveals errors.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · The career portfolio','The portfolio documents your research, work-based learning and preparation. Follow the guidelines exactly for required sections, format and length.',[
  ['Building portfolio content','A typical portfolio includes a description of the career, education and credential requirements, a summary of research evidence, a work-based learning experience such as shadowing or an interview, a description of the selected skill, and references. Write clearly and concisely in your own words, and cite every source. Organize the portfolio so a judge can find each required element quickly.','Research evidence means more than facts copied from a website: summarize findings about the career or skill, explain why they matter, and connect them to patient care. When describing work-based learning, include what you observed, what you learned and how it shaped your understanding. Proofread carefully; spelling and formatting errors suggest a lack of attention to detail, which matters in health care.',['A draft portfolio lists many facts but does not explain them. How should it be revised?','For each key fact, add an explanation of why it matters to the career or patient care, connect it to your work-based learning, and remove facts that do not support your message. Cite each source.'],[['Required elements','Every guideline section must be present and easy to find.',['required','section','element']],['Research evidence','Summarize, explain and connect findings to care.',['research','evidence','explain']],['Work-based learning','Reflect on what you observed and learned.',['shadow','interview','learn']]],['What makes research evidence stronger in a portfolio?',['Explaining why findings matter and citing sources','Copying long passages','Listing facts without context','Omitting citations'],'Analysis and citations show understanding.'],['A work-based learning reflection should include:',['What you observed, learned and how it shaped your view','Only the date','Only the professional’s salary','Nothing personal'],'Reflection connects experience to learning.']],
  ['Citations, design and professionalism','Use a consistent citation style, such as APA, for in-text citations and the reference list. Design should support reading: clear headings, consistent fonts, adequate white space and accurate captions for any images. Use only images you have permission to use or have created. Protect privacy: do not include patient names, photos or identifying details from shadowing experiences.','Professionalism in a portfolio means accurate information, honest representation of your experiences, and respect for confidentiality. Claiming experiences you did not have is dishonest and may lead to disqualification. Have a teacher or mentor review the portfolio against the rating sheet before submission, and check file format and size requirements for online submission.',['During shadowing you took notes about a patient’s condition. How should you include this experience in your portfolio ethically?','Describe what you learned in general terms without names, dates, photos or details that could identify the patient. Focus on the professional’s role and skills you observed, protecting confidentiality.'],[['Citation style','Consistent in-text citations and references.',['citation','apa','reference']],['Confidentiality','No identifying patient information.',['confiden','privacy','identif']],['Rating sheet review','Check work against the official criteria.',['rating','criteria','review']]],['Which should never appear in a portfolio about shadowing?',['A patient’s name or identifying photo','A general description of skills observed','The professional’s role','What you learned'],'Patient privacy must be protected.'],['Why review the portfolio against the rating sheet?',['To ensure every judged criterion is addressed','To make it longer','To change the career','To avoid citations'],'The rating sheet shows how points are awarded.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Recording the skill demonstration','The recorded skill must follow the guidelines for length, format and content. Judges evaluate technique, safety and communication as shown on video.',[
  ['Planning the recording','Write a shot plan before recording: what appears in frame, where the camera sits, and how each step will be visible. Use good lighting, a stable camera and clear audio. Keep the background uncluttered and professional. Wear appropriate attire for the career and follow infection-control practices on camera, because judges can only score what they see and hear.','Narrate or show each step clearly so judges understand your actions. Check the guidelines for time limits and whether editing is allowed. Do a full test recording and watch it as a judge would, using the rating sheet. Confirm the file plays correctly on the device you will bring and that you have a backup.',['After your first recording, you notice your hands block the view of a key step. What should you change?','Reposition the camera or your body so the step is visible, add a second angle if permitted, and re-record. Judges cannot award points for steps they cannot see.'],[['Shot plan','Plan what each part of the video shows.',['shot','plan','frame']],['Visibility','Judges can score only what is clearly visible.',['visib','see','camera']],['Test recording','Review a full practice video with the rating sheet.',['test','review','rating']]],['Why create a shot plan before recording?',['To ensure each step is visible and organized','To make the video longer than allowed','To avoid practicing','To skip safety steps'],'Planning makes the demonstration clear.'],['Which is most important for audio quality?',['Recording in a quiet space close to the microphone','Recording near loud machines','Speaking very fast','Using background music only'],'Clear audio lets judges follow narration.']],
  ['Demonstrating safely and accurately','A strong demonstration shows correct sequence, safe technique, infection control and respectful patient communication. Introduce yourself, identify the patient, explain the procedure and check comfort, just as you would with a real patient. Use a mannequin when appropriate and never perform invasive procedures on a volunteer that are not permitted by the guidelines.','Errors on video cannot be explained away later, so practice until performance is consistent. If an error occurs during recording, re-record rather than submitting a flawed demonstration. Close the procedure properly: ensure the patient is safe and comfortable, dispose of materials correctly, perform hand hygiene and document or describe documentation.',['During a recording you forget to perform hand hygiene before touching the patient. What should you do?','Stop and re-record the demonstration from the beginning, including hand hygiene, because a missed infection-control step affects scoring and models unsafe practice.'],[['Patient communication','Introduce, identify, explain and check comfort.',['introduc','explain','comfort']],['Safe technique','Follow permitted, safe procedures.',['safe','technique','permit']],['Proper closure','Comfort, disposal, hand hygiene and documentation.',['closure','dispos','document']]],['Which communication step belongs at the start of a skill demonstration?',['Introduce yourself and explain the procedure','Collect payment','Skip patient identification','Begin without speaking'],'Communication establishes consent and trust.'],['If a critical step is missed during recording, the best choice is to:',['Re-record the demonstration','Submit it anyway','Cut the video randomly','Ignore the guidelines'],'A complete, correct demonstration earns points.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · The live presentation','The presentation to judges connects your career research, portfolio and skill. Confident, organized speaking and thoughtful answers to questions make a strong final impression.',[
  ['Structuring the presentation','Organize the presentation with an engaging opening, a clear overview of the career, highlights of your research and work-based learning, an explanation of the skill’s purpose and importance, and a memorable close. Follow the time limit exactly. Practice with a timer and adjust content to fit, prioritizing points that match the rating sheet.','Use your own words and speak to the judges rather than reading. Visual aids, if allowed, should support your message, not replace it. Transitions help judges follow your organization. Rehearse in front of others and ask for feedback on clarity, pace and eye contact.',['Your presentation runs two minutes over the limit. How should you revise it?','Remove details that do not support rating sheet criteria, combine related points, and shorten examples. Rehearse with a timer until the presentation fits comfortably within the limit.'],[['Clear structure','Opening, career overview, research, skill and close.',['structure','opening','close']],['Time management','Practice with a timer to meet the limit.',['time','limit','timer']],['Audience focus','Speak to judges rather than reading notes.',['judge','speak','eye']]],['What should guide which content to keep in a timed presentation?',['The rating sheet criteria','Whatever is most entertaining','Random selection','The longest sources'],'Criteria show what judges score.'],['Which practice most improves delivery?',['Rehearsing aloud with feedback','Reading silently once','Memorizing only the first sentence','Avoiding practice'],'Spoken rehearsal improves fluency.']],
  ['Answering judges’ questions and professionalism','Judges may ask about your career choice, research, skill or what you learned. Listen to the full question, pause briefly to think, and answer directly with specific examples. If you do not know an answer, say so honestly and explain how you would find out. Professional appearance, posture, eye contact and courtesy contribute to the overall impression.','Prepare by listing likely questions and practicing answers aloud: why this career, what challenged you, how the skill affects patient care, and what you would do next. Connect answers to evidence from your portfolio. Thank the judges at the end. Reflect afterward on what went well and what to improve.',['A judge asks a question you cannot answer. How should you respond professionally?','Acknowledge honestly that you are not sure, share what you do know if relevant, and explain how you would find the answer, such as consulting a professional or reliable reference.'],[['Direct answers','Listen fully and respond with specific examples.',['answer','specific','listen']],['Honesty','Admit uncertainty and explain how to find out.',['honest','uncertain','find']],['Professional presence','Appearance, posture, eye contact and courtesy.',['professional','posture','courtesy']]],['When you do not know the answer to a judge’s question, you should:',['Admit it honestly and explain how you would find out','Make up an answer','Change the subject','Remain silent'],'Honesty is a core professional value.'],['Which is a good way to prepare for judges’ questions?',['Practice answering likely questions aloud','Memorize the rating sheet only','Avoid thinking about questions','Read the portfolio silently once'],'Rehearsal builds confident, specific answers.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Dental Science',[
 ['Unit 1 · Dental anatomy, pathology and tooth morphology','Dental anatomy and physiology, dental pathology and tooth morphology each make up 10% of the Dental Science test plan. These foundations support chairside assisting, radiology and emergency care.',[
  ['Oral anatomy and tooth structure','The oral cavity includes the lips, cheeks, tongue, hard and soft palate, salivary glands and teeth. Salivary glands, the parotid, submandibular and sublingual, produce saliva that begins digestion and protects teeth. Each tooth has a crown and root. Enamel is the hardest substance in the body and covers the crown; dentin forms the bulk of the tooth; cementum covers the root; and the pulp contains nerves and blood vessels.','The periodontium supports teeth: gingiva, periodontal ligament, cementum and alveolar bone. Adults normally have 32 permanent teeth including third molars, and children have 20 primary teeth. The Universal Numbering System numbers permanent teeth 1 to 32 starting with the maxillary right third molar, and primary teeth A to T. Tooth surfaces are named mesial, distal, facial (buccal or labial), lingual, and occlusal or incisal.',['Practice: a dental chart notes decay on the mesial surface of tooth #30. Identify the tooth and describe the location.','Tooth #30 is the mandibular right first molar. The mesial surface faces toward the midline of the dental arch, the front side of that molar.'],[['Tooth layers','Enamel, dentin, cementum and pulp.',['enamel','dentin','pulp']],['Numbering system','Universal system: 1 to 32 permanent, A to T primary.',['number','universal','32']],['Tooth surfaces','Mesial, distal, facial, lingual and occlusal.',['mesial','distal','surface']]],['Which is the hardest substance in the human body?',['Enamel','Dentin','Bone','Cementum'],'Enamel is highly mineralized.'],['How many primary teeth do children normally have?',['20','32','28','24'],'There are 20 primary teeth, lettered A to T.']],
  ['Dental pathology','Dental caries is a bacterial disease in which acids produced from sugars demineralize tooth structure. Gingivitis is reversible inflammation of the gums, while periodontitis involves loss of supporting bone and attachment. Other conditions include malocclusion, abscesses, oral candidiasis and oral cancer. Risk factors for oral cancer include tobacco and heavy alcohol use, and some cases are linked to human papillomavirus.','Fluoride strengthens enamel and helps remineralize early lesions. Sealants protect pits and fissures of molars. Plaque that is not removed can harden into calculus, which requires professional removal. Warning signs that need evaluation include persistent sores, red or white patches, bleeding gums and loose teeth. Regular exams detect problems early when treatment is simpler.',['Practice: a patient’s gums bleed when brushing but there is no bone loss on radiographs. What condition is likely, and is it reversible?','The findings suggest gingivitis, inflammation limited to the gums. It is generally reversible with improved oral hygiene and professional cleaning, before progressing to periodontitis.'],[['Caries','Acid from bacteria demineralizes tooth structure.',['caries','acid','bacteria']],['Gingivitis vs periodontitis','Reversible gum inflammation versus loss of support.',['gingivitis','periodontitis','bone']],['Prevention','Fluoride, sealants and plaque removal.',['fluoride','sealant','plaque']]],['Which condition involves loss of supporting bone around teeth?',['Periodontitis','Gingivitis','Caries only','Halitosis only'],'Periodontitis destroys attachment and bone.'],['What hardened deposit forms from untreated plaque?',['Calculus','Enamel','Dentin','Cementum'],'Mineralized plaque becomes calculus.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Infection control and occupational safety','Infection control and occupational health and safety make up 10% of the test plan. Dental settings involve aerosols, sharps and chemicals, so systematic precautions protect patients and staff.',[
  ['Infection control in dentistry','Standard precautions apply to every patient. Dental personnel wear gloves, masks, eye protection and protective clothing. Instruments are classified by infection risk: critical items that penetrate tissue must be sterilized, semi-critical items that contact mucous membranes are sterilized or receive high-level disinfection, and non-critical items that touch intact skin are cleaned and disinfected. Dental unit waterlines must be maintained to control bacterial biofilm.','Instrument processing follows a workflow from contaminated to clean areas: cleaning, packaging, sterilization, storage and delivery. Sterilization is monitored with mechanical, chemical and biological indicators; biological indicators using bacterial spores provide the best confirmation. Surfaces are cleaned and disinfected between patients or protected with barriers. Hand hygiene occurs before and after gloving.',['Practice: a biological indicator from the autoclave shows growth. What does this mean and what should happen next?','Growth means sterilization may have failed. Remove the sterilizer from service, recall and reprocess instruments from that load as required by policy, check the sterilizer, and retest before resuming use.'],[['Spaulding classification','Critical, semi-critical and non-critical items.',['critical','semi','classif']],['Sterilization monitoring','Mechanical, chemical and biological indicators.',['indicator','biolog','spore']],['Instrument workflow','Move from contaminated to clean areas.',['workflow','clean','package']]],['A scaler that penetrates soft tissue is classified as:',['Critical','Non-critical','Semi-critical','Housekeeping'],'Items that penetrate tissue must be sterilized.'],['Which indicator provides the best confirmation of sterilization?',['Biological indicator','Color of the package only','Room temperature','Instrument weight'],'Spore tests show whether conditions killed resistant organisms.']],
  ['Occupational health and safety','OSHA standards protect dental workers. The Bloodborne Pathogens Standard requires an exposure control plan, PPE, sharps containers and offering hepatitis B vaccination. The Hazard Communication Standard requires labeled chemicals and accessible safety data sheets. Ergonomics, proper positioning and instrument grasp reduce musculoskeletal injuries. Radiation safety protects workers and patients during imaging.','After a needlestick, wash the area with soap and water, report immediately, and follow the post-exposure evaluation plan. Never recap needles with two hands; use a one-handed scoop technique or safety device. Mercury from amalgam scrap must be stored and disposed of according to regulations. Training and documentation demonstrate compliance and protect everyone.',['Practice: a dental assistant is stuck by a used needle while cleaning a tray. Describe the immediate steps.','Wash the wound with soap and water, report the exposure to the supervisor right away, document the incident, and follow the post-exposure plan for evaluation and possible preventive treatment.'],[['Bloodborne Pathogens Standard','Exposure control plan, PPE and hepatitis B vaccine.',['bloodborne','plan','hepatitis']],['Hazard communication','Labels and safety data sheets for chemicals.',['hazard','label','sds']],['Sharps safety','One-handed recapping and safety devices.',['sharps','needle','recap']]],['Which document describes the hazards of a chemical?',['Safety data sheet','Appointment card','Treatment plan','Dental chart'],'SDSs provide hazard and handling information.'],['How should a used needle be recapped if necessary?',['With a one-handed scoop technique or safety device','With two hands quickly','By a patient','Never disposed of'],'One-handed methods reduce needlestick risk.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Chairside assisting, instruments and materials','Chairside assisting and instruments, including restorative and laboratory materials, are the largest domain at 30% of the test plan.',[
  ['Four-handed dentistry and instruments','Four-handed dentistry is a technique in which the operator and assistant work together efficiently, with the assistant transferring instruments, maintaining a clear field and anticipating needs. Operator zones are described using a clock face. Basic examination instruments include the mouth mirror, explorer and cotton pliers. Hand-cutting instruments include excavators and chisels; restorative instruments include amalgam carriers, condensers, burnishers and carvers.','Instruments are arranged on trays in order of use, usually from left to right. Instrument transfer occurs in the transfer zone below the patient’s chin, with the assistant delivering the instrument in the position of use. High-volume evacuation removes fluids and debris, while the saliva ejector removes small amounts. Isolation with a dental dam improves visibility and moisture control.',['Practice: during a restoration the operator needs better visibility and less moisture. What can the assistant do?','Position the high-volume evacuator to remove fluids and debris, use the air-water syringe as needed, retract tissue, and, if planned, help place a dental dam for isolation.'],[['Four-handed dentistry','Efficient teamwork between operator and assistant.',['four-handed','team','assist']],['Instrument sequence','Trays are arranged in order of use.',['order','tray','sequence']],['Moisture control','High-volume evacuation and isolation improve the field.',['evacuat','moisture','dam']]],['Which instrument is used to detect irregularities on tooth surfaces?',['Explorer','Amalgam carrier','Burnisher','Saliva ejector'],'The explorer helps detect caries and irregularities.'],['Instruments on a tray are typically arranged:',['In order of use','Randomly','By color','By price'],'Order of use supports efficient transfer.']],
  ['Restorative and laboratory materials','Restorative materials repair tooth structure. Composite resin is tooth-colored and bonded to tooth structure, often light-cured. Amalgam is a metal alloy that is durable but not tooth-colored. Glass ionomer releases fluoride and bonds chemically to tooth structure. Laboratory materials include impression materials, such as alginate and elastomers, and gypsum products used to make study models and casts.','Material handling affects results: follow manufacturer mixing times and ratios. Alginate impressions should be poured promptly because they lose or absorb water and distort. Water temperature affects alginate setting time; warmer water speeds setting. Moisture control is critical for bonded composites. Gypsum products differ in strength, with dental stone stronger than plaster.',['Practice: an alginate impression sits on the counter for an hour before being poured, and the model does not fit well. Explain why.','Alginate loses moisture over time, a process that causes shrinkage and distortion. Delayed pouring produced an inaccurate model; impressions should be poured promptly or stored according to manufacturer guidance.'],[['Composite resin','Tooth-colored, bonded and light-cured.',['composite','bond','cure']],['Alginate handling','Pour promptly to avoid distortion.',['alginate','pour','distort']],['Gypsum products','Plaster and stone differ in strength for models.',['gypsum','stone','plaster']]],['Which restorative material releases fluoride?',['Glass ionomer','Amalgam only','Stainless steel','Wax'],'Glass ionomer releases fluoride over time.'],['Using warmer water when mixing alginate will:',['Speed up setting time','Slow setting dramatically','Prevent setting','Change its color only'],'Higher temperature accelerates setting.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Radiology and medical emergencies','Radiology and medical emergencies in the dental office each make up 10% of the test plan. Both demand careful technique and preparation.',[
  ['Dental radiography and radiation safety','Dental radiographs reveal caries, bone levels, infections and developing teeth. Intraoral images include periapical views of the entire tooth and root, bitewings for interproximal caries and bone levels, and occlusal views. Panoramic images show both jaws. Radiolucent areas appear dark because radiation passes through, while radiopaque areas such as enamel and metal restorations appear light.','Radiation safety follows ALARA, as low as reasonably achievable. Use the fewest exposures needed based on clinical judgment, fast image receptors or digital sensors, rectangular collimation, and proper technique to avoid retakes. Operators stand behind a barrier or at a safe distance and angle. Positioning errors such as cone cutting or elongation reduce diagnostic value and may require retakes.',['Practice: a bitewing image shows a dark area between two molars. What might it indicate, and why are bitewings useful here?','A radiolucent area between teeth may indicate interproximal caries. Bitewings show the contacts and crowns of upper and lower posterior teeth, making them well suited to detect decay between teeth.'],[['Image types','Periapical, bitewing, occlusal and panoramic.',['periapical','bitewing','panoramic']],['Radiolucent vs radiopaque','Dark areas versus light areas on images.',['radiolucent','radiopaque','dark']],['ALARA','Keep exposure as low as reasonably achievable.',['alara','exposure','low']]],['Which image best detects decay between posterior teeth?',['Bitewing','Panoramic only','Occlusal','Extraoral photograph'],'Bitewings show interproximal surfaces.'],['A metal restoration appears on a radiograph as:',['Radiopaque (light)','Radiolucent (dark)','Invisible','Blue'],'Metal absorbs radiation and appears light.']],
  ['Medical emergencies in the dental office','Prevention starts with a complete medical history and vital signs. Syncope, or fainting, is the most common dental office emergency and is managed by positioning the patient supine with legs elevated and monitoring breathing. Other emergencies include hypoglycemia, asthma attacks, allergic reactions including anaphylaxis, seizures, angina and cardiac arrest. Offices maintain an emergency kit, oxygen and an automated external defibrillator.','Each team member should know their role: one person stays with the patient, one calls emergency services, and one brings the kit and AED. Anaphylaxis may cause hives, swelling, breathing difficulty and low blood pressure, and is treated with epinephrine according to protocol and training. Emergency drills keep the team ready, and events are documented afterward.',['Practice: a patient becomes pale, sweaty and lightheaded during a local anesthetic injection and then loses consciousness briefly. What is likely and how is it managed?','This suggests vasovagal syncope. Stop the procedure, place the patient supine with legs elevated, ensure an open airway, monitor breathing and pulse, and provide oxygen per protocol. Activate emergency services if the patient does not recover promptly.'],[['Medical history','Prevention begins with history and vital signs.',['history','vital','prevent']],['Syncope management','Supine position with legs elevated and monitoring.',['syncope','supine','elevat']],['Team roles','Assigned roles speed emergency response.',['role','team','aed']]],['What is the most common medical emergency in the dental office?',['Syncope','Cardiac arrest','Stroke','Seizure'],'Fainting is the most frequent office emergency.'],['A patient develops hives, swelling and difficulty breathing after a medication. This suggests:',['Anaphylaxis','Syncope','Hypoglycemia','Angina'],'These signs indicate a severe allergic reaction.']]
 ]]
]);
